// @test: run
// @tier: 1
// @level: L0
// @tags: aot, codegen, shared, wrapper, field-access, regression
// @timeout: 30000
// @expected-exit: 0
// @expected-stdout-begin
// a=1111
// b=2222
// @expected-stdout-end
//
// Regression guard for T0393: `s.field` on a `Shared<T>` must reach the
// WRAPPED value, not the wrapper's own fields.
//
// TIER 1 ONLY, and that is deliberate rather than an omission: the
// interpreter has its OWN defect in this same syntax, owned by T1202. A
// `differential` spec here would fail on the interpreter for a reason
// this file is not about — which is how a regression guard starts
// reporting somebody else's defect and then gets muted.
//
// THIS HEADER PREVIOUSLY SAID SOMETHING FALSE and the retraction is
// kept because the false version is the more tempting reading. It said
// the two tiers hold DIFFERENT OBJECTS — that AOT has the declared
// record while the interpreter substitutes a carrier — and offered as
// evidence that the interpreter's slot 0 reads as `1`, which `ptr`
// cannot be. The raw dump disproves it:
//
//     slot 0 = 0x7ff9000af6c525a0     Int-TAGGED, i.e. `ptr`
//     slot 1 = 1                      `generation` — and this is the
//                                     `1` I had attributed to slot 0,
//                                     reached by an `.add(1)` peel
//
// Both tiers hold the same declared `Shared { ptr, generation, epoch }`.
// I read a NaN-boxed address as a small integer, and built a two-object
// theory on the unparsed tag. The two fixes are still separate, but
// because the two PEELS are separately wrong — not because the two
// tiers disagree about what a `Shared` IS.
//
// WHAT IT WOULD CATCH. Before the fix, `s.a` and `s.b` read fields 0 and
// 1 of `Shared<T> { ptr, generation, epoch }`:
//
//     a = 4445323296     the pointer, printed as a decimal
//     b = 1              the generation
//
// with **rc = 0**. The task's July acceptance said "no SIGSEGV", and by
// September there was no SIGSEGV and also no correct answer — an
// acceptance keyed on a symptom goes false-green the moment the defect
// changes costume. Hence the expected output above names the VALUES.
//
// AND IT PINS A CONSTANT. `SHARED_INNER_VALUE_OFFSET = 16` in
// `verum_codegen/src/llvm/instruction.rs` is the byte offset of
// `SharedInner<T>.value` — `{ strong_count: UInt32, weak_count: UInt32,
// value: T }`, slot 2, in a HEADERLESS `cbgr_alloc` block
// (`FIELDADDR-HEADERLESS-1`, T1159). If `core/base/memory.vr` reorders
// those fields and the constant is not moved with it, `a=` and `b=`
// below stop matching. That is the whole point of asserting the field
// VALUES rather than merely that the program survives.
//
// THE `s.deref()` CONTROL WAS REMOVED, and why is worth keeping. It was
// here to say "if this fails, the fault is in the stdlib body and not in
// this lowering" — and it failed, on the fixed binary AND on the one
// before it. Isolated to a single line, and it is not this defect:
//
//     fn main()        -> deref=1111
//     fn main() -> Int -> deref=0        both binaries
//
// Changing main's RETURN TYPE changes the value a `Shared` deref
// produces. That is filed separately with the two-line repro. A spec
// must fail for exactly one reason, and a control that measures a
// second defect is a control that will be muted rather than read.

mount core.prelude.*;
mount core.base.memory.{Shared};

type Q is { a: Int, b: Int };

fn main() -> Int {
    let s = Shared.new(Q { a: 1111, b: 2222 });

    // The two field reads this task is about.
    print(f"a={s.a}");
    print(f"b={s.b}");

    0
}
